CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional diagram of a cube sitting in the uniform field between two oppositely charged plates. Two large grey parallelograms are drawn in perspective, one above and one below, labeled '-q' at the right edge of the upper plate and '+q' at the right edge of the lower one; both are covered with short red arrows that all point straight up, and the field itself is named with a vector E at the far left. Between the plates stands a cube with every vertex lettered, A, B, C and D around the bottom face and F, G, H and K around the top, so the bottom face reads ABCD and the top face FGHK. A shaded diagonal plane is drawn inside the cube, running from the upper edge KF down to the lower edge BC and dividing the cube into two wedges. Red field arrows continue through the cube's interior, entering the bottom face and leaving the top face, while the four vertical side faces lie parallel to them. Because the identical arrows enter below and exit above, the reader is meant to tally signed contributions face by face and see them cancel. The arrangement is used to evaluate the flux through a closed surface that encloses no charge.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Upper charged plate drawn as a large grey parallelogram (receding to the upper-left), labeled '-q' at its right edge
  - Lower charged plate drawn as an identical grey parallelogram below, labeled '+q' at its right edge
  - Dense grid of short red upward-pointing arrows covering the upper plate surface
  - Dense grid of short red upward-pointing arrows covering the lower plate surface
  - Vector label 'E' with arrow symbol at the far left, at the level of the upper plate's lower edge
  - Wireframe cube standing midway between the plates, edges drawn as thin dark grey lines
  - Bottom cube face ABCD with vertex labels A (front-left), B (front-right), C (back-right), D (back-left)
  - Top cube face FGHK with vertex labels F (front-left), G (front-right), H (back-right), K (back-left)
  - Four vertical side faces (ABGF, BCHG, CDKH, DAFK) shown as transparent planes parallel to the field
  - Shaded interior diagonal plane running from the top-left edge KF down to the bottom-right edge BC, splitting the cube into two wedges
  - Column of red field arrows passing vertically through the cube interior, entering the bottom face and exiting the top face
  - Red field arrows in the open space between the plates and the cube

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: cube side 2 units centered at the origin, vertices at (±1, ±1, ±1). Bottom face ABCD at y=-1 with A=(-1,-1,+1) front-left, B=(+1,-1,+1) front-right, C=(+1,-1,-1) back-right, D=(-1,-1,-1) back-left; top face FGHK at y=+1 with F above A, G above B, H above C, K above D. Labels sit just outside each vertex in dark grey serif italic. Plates: two flat parallelograms in the horizontal planes y=+3.4 (labeled '-q' at its right corner) and y=-3.4 (labeled '+q'), each spanning about 11 units in X and 6 units in Z, drawn as grey gradient sheets (#d9d9d9 → #f0f0f0) with thin grey borders; the receding direction offsets them to the upper-left on screen. Each plate carries roughly a 10×6 grid of short red arrows (length ~0.5, shaft width ~0.05) all pointing along +Y. About 12-16 identical red arrows also run through the free space and straight through the cube interior, entering the bottom face and exiting the top. The bold 'E' vector label with a small up-arrow sits at the far left, just below the upper plate's near edge. Interior diagonal plane is the quadrilateral K(-1,1,-1) → F(-1,1,1) → B(1,-1,1) → C(1,-1,-1): a pale cyan-grey translucent sheet (#cfe4e2, opacity ~0.45) tilting down from the top-left edge to the bottom-right edge. Palette: arrows crimson #c0392b, cube edges #4a4a4a, faces near-transparent white with faint cyan tint (#dff0ee, opacity 0.25), label text #222. Physics: E = E ŷ with E arbitrary (use E = 1), face area A = 4. Φ_top = +EA = +4, Φ_bottom = −EA = −4, Φ_side = 0 for all four sides; net Φ = 0 = q_enc/ε₀. Color code face highlights: positive flux warm red-orange #e05a3c, negative flux blue #3b6fb5, zero flux neutral grey #9a9a9a.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The cube is a closed surface in a 3D uniform field; rotating reveals which faces the arrows pierce and which they merely graze."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectFace",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Each face carries a signed flux E·n̂A: the top face is positive (field leaves), the bottom negative (field enters), and the four vertical side faces are zero because their normals are perpendicular to E."
  },
  {
    "id": "fluxView",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Flux view",
